---
/** The weekly target, and the personal heart-rate band behind it. Rendered by /science/movement/zone-2; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";
import {
  ZONE2_SECTION,
  ZONE2_HR_SECTION,
} from "../../data/science";
---

<div class="mt-[18px] flex flex-col gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-ink/8 bg-ink/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px] lg:flex-row lg:items-center lg:gap-[18px]">
  <div class="flex-1">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">In the app</p>
    <p class="mt-2.5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
      Your band is Karvonen, built from your own resting rate and your
      age-predicted maximum: 40 to 60 percent of the reserve between them.
      The app counts minutes inside that band, not inside a table everyone
      shares.
    </p>
  </div>
  <!-- Sample figures: one reader's resting rate, band and maximum. -->
  <div class="w-full shrink-0 lg:w-[220px]">
    <div class="flex h-[9px] overflow-hidden rounded-full">
      <span class="w-2/5 bg-ink/8"></span>
      <span class="w-1/5 bg-success/55"></span>
      <span class="w-2/5 bg-ink/8"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-[7px] flex justify-between font-mono text-[10px]">
      <span class="text-text-muted">52</span>
      <span class="text-success">102&ndash;127</span>
      <span class="text-text-muted">177</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="zone-2" /></div>
<h3 id="zone-2-from-a-test" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">If you have a test</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  The band above is an estimate: 40 to 60% of your heart-rate reserve, from your resting and
  your age-predicted maximum heart rate. A cardiopulmonary exercise test (CPET) or a lactate
  test measures where your own first and second thresholds fall, and they sit wherever your
  physiology puts them, not where a formula does. If you have one, enter its two Zone 2 heart
  rates in the app under Settings &rarr; Profile &rarr; <strong class="text-text-primary">Zone 2 from a
  test</strong>. From then on every band in Verve is cut at those two rates: Zone 2 minutes, the
  light, moderate and vigorous split, and the Weekly dose. The Zone 2 tile says the band came
  from your test. Leave both rates blank to go back to the estimate.
</p>
<div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {ZONE2_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>
<h3 id="heart-rate-method-karvonen-hrr" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Heart-rate method: Karvonen HRR</h3>
<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  "Zone 2" is a training label applied to the 40&ndash;60% Heart Rate Reserve
  band &mdash; different models (Seiler polarised, Maffetone 180-minus-age,
  San Mill&aacute;n VT1-based) draw the upper boundary slightly differently, but
  all target the same physiological space just below the first ventilatory
  threshold.
</p>
<div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {ZONE2_HR_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>

<h3 id="the-three-zones" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Zone 1 and Zone 3: either side of the band</h3>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Verve draws one band, Zone 2, and names what falls either side of it. Zone 1 is every minute
  of a logged session below the band: under 40% of your heart-rate reserve. Zone 3 is every
  minute above it: over 60%. If you entered a test, the two cuts are your test's two heart
  rates instead. The three sum to the session, so no minute is left unexplained.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  The three rows count logged aerobic sessions only. What you do outside a session has its own
  row, Background movement, and is never folded into the zones.
  <a href="/how-it-works#background-movement" class="font-semibold text-accent-light hover:text-text-primary">How background movement is read &rarr;</a>
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Why Zone 1 has no target.</strong> The
  <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33239350/" class="font-semibold text-accent-light hover:text-text-primary">WHO 2020 guidelines</a>
  set their weekly amounts in moderate and vigorous minutes: 150 to 300 moderate, or 75 to 150
  vigorous. They also say that some activity is better than none, and they set no weekly
  amount for lighter movement. So Verve reports your Zone 1 minutes and grades nothing by them.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">What a Zone 3 minute counts for.</strong> In the same
  guidelines the vigorous amounts are half the moderate ones: a harder minute stands for more.
  Verve's weekly dose does this without a rule of thumb. It multiplies each minute by its METs,
  so a Zone 3 minute adds more than a Zone 2 minute, by as much as it was harder.
</p>
<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <strong class="text-text-primary">Not the laboratory's three zones.</strong> Endurance
  research often uses three zones cut at the two thresholds a lactate or breathing test finds
  (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20861519/" class="font-semibold text-accent-light hover:text-text-primary">Seiler, 2010</a>).
  Verve's three are cut around its Zone 2 band by heart-rate reserve, unless you enter a test.
  The names match and the cuts do not, so minutes in one scheme are not minutes in the other.
</p>
